FINDMNT(8)                   System Administration                  FINDMNT(8)



NNAAMMEE
       findmnt - find a filesystem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ffiinnddmmnntt [options]

       ffiinnddmmnntt [options] _d_e_v_i_c_e|_m_o_u_n_t_p_o_i_n_t

       ffiinnddmmnntt [options] [----ssoouurrccee] _d_e_v_i_c_e [----ttaarrggeett _p_a_t_h|----mmoouunnttppooiinntt
       _m_o_u_n_t_p_o_i_n_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ffiinnddmmnntt will list all mounted filesystems or search for a filesystem.
       The ffiinnddmmnntt command is able to search in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b, _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b or
       _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o. If _d_e_v_i_c_e or _m_o_u_n_t_p_o_i_n_t is not given, all
       filesystems are shown.

       The device may be specified by device name, major:minor numbers,
       filesystem label or UUID, or partition label or UUID. Note that ffiinnddmmnntt
       follows mmoouunntt(8) behavior where a device name may be interpreted as a
       mountpoint (and vice versa) if the ----ttaarrggeett, ----mmoouunnttppooiinntt or ----ssoouurrccee
       options are not specified.

       The command-line option ----ttaarrggeett accepts any file or directory and then
       ffiinnddmmnntt displays the filesystem for the given path.

       The command prints all mounted filesystems in the tree-like format by
       default.

       The relationship between block devices and filesystems is not always
       one-to-one. The filesystem may use more block devices. This is why
       ffiinnddmmnntt provides  SOURCE and SOURCES (pl.) columns. The column SOURCES
       displays all devices where it is possible to find the same filesystem
       UUID (or another tag specified in _f_s_t_a_b when executed with ----ffssttaabb and
       ----eevvaalluuaattee).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --AA, ----aallll
           Disable all built-in filters and print all filesystems.

       --aa, ----aasscciiii
           Use ascii characters for tree formatting.

       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print the sizes in bytes rather than in a human-readable format.

           By default, the unit, sizes are expressed in, is byte, and unit
           prefixes are in power of 2^10 (1024). Abbreviations of symbols are
           exhibited truncated in order to reach a better readability, by
           exhibiting alone the first letter of them; examples: "1 KiB" and "1
           MiB" are respectively exhibited as "1 K" and "1 M", then omitting
           on purpose the mention "iB", which is part of these abbreviations.

       --CC, ----nnooccaannoonniiccaalliizzee
           Do not canonicalize paths at all. This option affects the comparing
           of paths and the evaluation of tags (LABEL, UUID, etc.).

       --cc, ----ccaannoonniiccaalliizzee
           Canonicalize all printed paths.

       ----ddeelleetteedd
           Print filesystems where target (mountpoint) is marked as deleted by
           kernel.

       --DD, ----ddff
           Imitate the output of ddff(1). This option is equivalent to --oo
           SSOOUURRCCEE,,FFSSTTYYPPEE,,SSIIZZEE,,UUSSEEDD,,AAVVAAIILL,,UUSSEE%%,,TTAARRGGEETT but excludes all pseudo
           filesystems. Use ----aallll to print all filesystems.

       --dd, ----ddiirreeccttiioonn _w_o_r_d
           The search direction, either ffoorrwwaarrdd or bbaacckkwwaarrdd.

       --ee, ----eevvaalluuaattee
           Convert all tags (LABEL, UUID, PARTUUID, or PARTLABEL) to the
           corresponding device names for the SOURCE column. It’s an unusual
           situation, but the same tag may be duplicated (used for more
           devices). For this purpose, there is SOURCES (pl.) column. This
           column displays by multi-line cell all devices where the tag is
           detected by libblkid. This option makes sense for _f_s_t_a_b only.

       --FF, ----ttaabb--ffiillee _p_a_t_h
           Search in an alternative file. If used with ----ffssttaabb, ----mmttaabb or
           ----kkeerrnneell, then it overrides the default paths. If specified more
           than once, then tree-like output is disabled (see the ----lliisstt
           option).

       --ff, ----ffiirrsstt--oonnllyy
           Print the first matching filesystem only.

       --ii, ----iinnvveerrtt
           Invert the sense of matching.

       --JJ, ----jjssoonn
           Use JSON output format.

       --kk, ----kkeerrnneell
           Search in _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o. The output is in the tree-like
           format. This is the default. The output contains only mount options
           maintained by kernel (see also ----mmttaabb).

       --ll, ----lliisstt
           Use the list output format. This output format is automatically
           enabled if the output is restricted by the --tt, --OO, --SS or --TT option
           and the option ----ssuubbmmoouunnttss is not used or if more that one source
           file (the option --FF) is specified.

       --MM, ----mmoouunnttppooiinntt _p_a_t_h
           Explicitly define the mountpoint file or directory. See also
           ----ttaarrggeett.

       --mm, ----mmttaabb
           Search in _/_e_t_c_/_m_t_a_b. The output is in the list format by default
           (see ----ttrreeee). The output may include user space mount options.

       --NN, ----ttaasskk _t_i_d
           Use alternative namespace _/_p_r_o_c_/_<_t_i_d_>_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o rather than the
           default _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o. If the option is specified more than
           once, then tree-like output is disabled (see the ----lliisstt option).
           See also the uunnsshhaarree(1) command.

       --nn, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       --OO, ----ooppttiioonnss _l_i_s_t
           Limit the set of printed filesystems. More than one option may be
           specified in a comma-separated list. The --tt and --OO options are
           cumulative in effect. It is different from --tt in that each option
           is matched exactly; a leading _n_o at the beginning does not have
           global meaning. The "no" can used for individual items in the list.
           The "no" prefix interpretation can be disabled by "+" prefix.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Define output columns. See the ----hheellpp output to get a list of the
           currently supported columns. The TTAARRGGEETT column contains tree
           formatting if the ----lliisstt or ----rraaww options are not specified.

           The default list of columns may be extended if _l_i_s_t is specified in
           the format _+_l_i_s_t (e.g., ffiinnddmmnntt --oo ++PPRROOPPAAGGAATTIIOONN).

       ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output almost all available columns. The columns that require
           ----ppoollll are not included.

       --PP, ----ppaaiirrss
           Produce output in the form of key="value" pairs. All potentially
           unsafe value characters are hex-escaped (\x<code>). See also option
           ----sshheellll.

       --pp, ----ppoollll[_=_l_i_s_t]
           Monitor changes in the _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_e_l_f_/_m_o_u_n_t_i_n_f_o file. Supported actions
           are: mount, umount, remount and move. More than one action may be
           specified in a comma-separated list. All actions are monitored by
           default.

           The time for which ----ppoollll will block can be restricted with the
           ----ttiimmeeoouutt or ----ffiirrsstt--oonnllyy options.

           The standard columns always use the new version of the information
           from the mountinfo file, except the umount action which is based on
           the original information cached by ffiinnddmmnntt. The poll mode allows
           using extra columns:

           AACCTTIIOONN
               mount, umount, move or remount action name; this column is
               enabled by default

           OOLLDD--TTAARRGGEETT
               available for umount and move actions

           OOLLDD--OOPPTTIIOONNSS
               available for umount and remount actions

       ----ppsseeuuddoo
           Print only pseudo filesystems.

       ----sshhaaddooww
           Print only filesystems over-mounted by another filesystem.

       --RR, ----ssuubbmmoouunnttss
           Print recursively all submounts for the selected filesystems. The
           restrictions defined by options --tt, --OO, --SS, --TT and ----ddiirreeccttiioonn are
           not applied to submounts. All submounts are always printed in
           tree-like order. The option enables the tree-like output format by
           default. This option has no effect for ----mmttaabb or ----ffssttaabb.

       --rr, ----rraaww
           Use raw output format. All potentially unsafe characters are
           hex-escaped (\x<code>).

       ----rreeaall
           Print only real filesystems.

       --SS, ----ssoouurrccee _s_p_e_c
           Explicitly define the mount source. Supported specifications are
           _d_e_v_i_c_e, _m_a_j::_m_i_n, LLAABBEELL==_l_a_b_e_l, UUUUIIDD==_u_u_i_d, PPAARRTTLLAABBEELL==_l_a_b_e_l and
           PPAARRTTUUUUIIDD==_u_u_i_d.

       --ss, ----ffssttaabb
           Search in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b. The output is in the list format (see
           ----lliisstt).

       --TT, ----ttaarrggeett _p_a_t_h
           Define the mount target. If _p_a_t_h is not a mountpoint file or
           directory, then ffiinnddmmnntt checks the _p_a_t_h elements in reverse order
           to get the mountpoint (this feature is supported only when
           searching in kernel files and unsupported for ----ffssttaabb). It’s
           recommended to use the option ----mmoouunnttppooiinntt when checks of _p_a_t_h
           elements are unwanted and _p_a_t_h is a strictly specified mountpoint.

       --tt, ----ttyyppeess _l_i_s_t
           Limit the set of printed filesystems. More than one type may be
           specified in a comma-separated list. The list of filesystem types
           can be prefixed with nnoo to specify the filesystem types on which no
           action should be taken. For more details see mmoouunntt(8).

       ----ttrreeee
           Enable tree-like output if possible. The options is silently
           ignored for tables where is missing child-parent relation (e.g.,
           _f_s_t_a_b).

       ----sshhaaddoowweedd
           Print only filesystems over-mounted by another filesystem.

       --UU, ----uunniiqq
           Ignore filesystems with duplicate mount targets, thus effectively
           skipping over-mounted mount points.

       --uu, ----nnoottrruunnccaattee
           Do not truncate text in columns. The default is to not truncate the
           TTAARRGGEETT, SSOOUURRCCEE, UUUUIIDD, LLAABBEELL, PPAARRTTUUUUIIDD, PPAARRTTLLAABBEELL columns. This
           option disables text truncation also in all other columns.

       --vv, ----nnooffssrroooott
           Do not print a [/dir] in the SOURCE column for bind mounts or btrfs
           subvolumes.

       --ww, ----ttiimmeeoouutt _m_i_l_l_i_s_e_c_o_n_d_s
           Specify an upper limit on the time for which ----ppoollll will block, in
           milliseconds.

       --xx, ----vveerriiffyy
           Check mount table content. The default is to verify _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b
           parsability and usability. It’s possible to use this option also
           with ----ttaabb--ffiillee. It’s possible to specify source (device) or target
           (mountpoint) to filter mount table. The option ----vveerrbboossee forces
           ffiinnddmmnntt to print more details.

       ----vveerrbboossee
           Force ffiinnddmmnntt to print more information (----vveerriiffyy only for now).

       ----vvffss--aallll
           When used with VVFFSS--OOPPTTIIOONNSS column, print all VFS (fs-independent)
           flags. This option is designed for auditing purposes to list also
           default VFS kernel mount options which are normally not listed.

       --yy, ----sshheellll
           The column name will be modified to contain only characters allowed
           for shell variable identifiers. This is usable, for example, with
           ----ppaaiirrss. Note that this feature has been automatically enabled for
           ----ppaaiirrss in version 2.37, but due to compatibility issues, now it’s
           necessary to request this behavior by ----sshheellll.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       The exit value is 0 if there is something to display, or 1 on any error
       (for example if no filesystem is found based on the user’s filter
       specification, or the device path or mountpoint does not exist).

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       LLIIBBMMOOUUNNTT__FFSSTTAABB=<path>
